        "description": "<img src=\"https://assetsio.gnwcdn.com/CoD-MW4_i33XbuE.jpg?width=690&quality=85&format=jpg&auto=webp\" /> <p>It's easy to dislike Call of Duty. Aside from the annual cycle of development, marketing, and controversy that has defined the series identity for the past 22 years, <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2014/oct/22/call-of-duty-gaming-role-military-entertainment-complex\">the series has become an integral part of how video games prop up the military-industrial complex</a>. Whilst some of the stories - <a href=\"https://www.eurogamer.net/call-of-duty-black-ops-7-review\">especially 2025's Black Ops 7</a> - have deteriorated into the realms of science-fiction absurdity, many of the narratives you'll find in the Modern Warfare splinter of the series are more grounded in reality. When you're playing as semi-fictional UK and US troopers, taking part in illegal operations in foreign territory, wantonly executing people over differences in ideology, Call of Duty can feel gross: ",
